The new XGB is worse than RF and worse than your old XGB on the test machine.
Train error went up a lot (0.11 → 0.31 MAE), and test error also went up (0.54 → 0.76 MAE).
That means: with the new settings, you’ve basically underfit the problem. You made the model weaker, and it didn’t translate into better generalization.
Also notice the “overfitting gap” in MAE:
Old XGB gap: 0.5389 − 0.1106 ≈ 0.43
New XGB gap: 0.7569 − 0.3084 ≈ 0.45
So the gap is about the same. We didn’t even really reduce overfitting; we just made the whole model worse.

Given these numbers, the simplest conclusion:
✅ Your original XGBoost setup (the one with MAE=0.5389 | RMSE=0.7131 on test) is your best model so far for the held-out machine.

