\subsection{Experimental Setup}

We trained and evaluated regression models on a dataset collected from multiple machines. To assess generalization across hardware, we used a group-wise split by \texttt{machine\_id}: all machines except \texttt{ip-172-31-17-37} were used for training, and \texttt{ip-172-31-17-37} (a c6 machine) was held out as the test set. 

As a simple baseline, we predicted the mean of the training targets for all test samples. For the learned models, we trained a Random Forest regressor and an XGBoost regressor on the same feature set. Categorical features were one-hot encoded, while numerical features were passed through unchanged using a scikit-learn \texttt{ColumnTransformer} within a pipeline. Performance was evaluated using Mean Absolute Error (MAE) and Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) on both the training set (all non-held-out machines) and the held-out test machine.

\subsection{Results}

Table~\ref{tab:model_results} summarizes the performance of the baseline and tree-based models. The baseline model, which always predicts the mean of the training targets, performs poorly on the held-out machine (MAE $= 1.5296$, RMSE $= 1.9576$), indicating substantial variability in the target that can be captured by more expressive models.

Both Random Forest and XGBoost substantially outperform the baseline. The Random Forest model achieves MAE $= 0.1992$ and RMSE $= 0.3572$ on the training machines, and MAE $= 0.6493$ and RMSE $= 0.8308$ on the held-out machine, showing good predictive power but a noticeable train--test gap consistent with overfitting. 

XGBoost provides the best overall performance. With our base configuration (600 trees, maximum depth 6, learning rate 0.05, subsampling and column subsampling set to 0.8), XGBoost attains MAE $= 0.1106$ and RMSE $= 0.1718$ on the training machines, and MAE $= 0.5389$ and RMSE $= 0.7131$ on the held-out machine. This corresponds to an improvement of approximately 17\% in MAE and 14\% in RMSE on the test machine compared to the Random Forest. 

We also experimented with additional L1 regularization for XGBoost by setting \texttt{reg\_alpha} $= 0.1$. This configuration slightly increased both training and test errors (see Table~\ref{tab:model_results}) and did not improve generalization. Overall, XGBoost with the base configuration is the best-performing model for this task and generalizes better to unseen hardware than both the Random Forest and the baseline.
	